digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56698-w
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This research proposes that an optimized, reproducible faecal small-RNA sequencing pipeline can define a conserved core miRNA signature in healthy mice and sensitively reveal disease-related miRNA shifts, including wound-healing, barrier-integrity and fibrosis-associated miRNAs altered during chronic Trichuris muris infection, thereby providing a reliable non-invasive tool for studying intestinal physiology and intestinal pathology.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A reproducible faecal small-RNA sequencing workflow can connect non-invasive faecal miRNA measurements in healthy and infected mice to intestinal barrier, wound-healing, immune and fibrotic pathology processes."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) EV encapsulation enhances extracellular miRNA stability, and mature miRNAs are detectable extracellularly in bodily fluids including faeces."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) High-throughput small RNA sequencing has been used to examine faecal miRNA profiles, while understanding of reproducibility and stability remains limited."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The study applies small-RNA sequencing to longitudinal analysis of murine faecal miRNAs in healthy mice."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The T. muris chronic disease model involves epithelial disruption and immune-response processes that are regulated by miRNAs."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Chronic T. muris infection provides an intestinal pathology context in which faecal miRNA changes can be studied in relation to epithelial barrier disruption and immune responses."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) T. muris chronicity is associated with a Th1-type immune response, while resistant mice generate a Th2-polarised response that facilitates wound healing."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) In chronic T. muris infection, miRNAs may plausibly contribute to tissue-damage repair in a Th1-polarised disease setting, considered against Th2-associated wound-healing biology."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Mature miRNAs are detectable in faeces, and mammalian intestinal miRNAs have roles in barrier and immune responses."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Faecal miRNAs are intestinally relevant regulatory molecules because miRNAs regulate gene expression and intestinal miRNAs participate in barrier and immune-response biology."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Previous microarray and qPCR studies of faecal miRNAs, together with reports that altered miRNAs can serve as diagnostic tools in gastrointestinal cancers, show prior diagnostic interest in faecal miRNA alterations."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) MicroRNAs function as post-transcriptional regulators in animals."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Faecal miRNA profiles were recently examined using high-throughput small RNA sequencing."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Deduction: Given sequencing studies and heterogeneity, there remains limited understanding of reproducibility and stability."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) The study reports the first longitudinal analysis of murine faecal miRNAs in healthy mice."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) T. muris is a model of the human gastrointestinal whipworm causing chronic disease."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) T. muris pathogenesis disrupts epithelium and immune response, processes regulated by miRNAs."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) In T. muris infection, Th1-type immune response is associated with chronicity."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) Resistant mice generate a Th2-polarised response that facilitates wound healing."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Mature miRNAs can be detected extracellularly in bodily fluids."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Encapsulation within extracellular vesicles (EVs) enhances miRNA stability."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) In the mammalian intestine, miRNAs perform essential roles in barrier and immune response."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) Previous work employed microarrays and qPCRs to study faecal miRNAs."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) Alterations in specific miRNAs have been identified as diagnostic tools in GI cancers."];
